WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.163
Evidence work does not end after initial assessment or authorization support.

2
00:00:05.163 --> 00:00:12.561
Continuous monitoring documentation shows whether the security posture remains current as the system changes.

3
00:00:12.561 --> 00:00:30.053
It may include vulnerability scans, configuration changes, significant changes at a high level, incident records, access reviews, patch status, risk updates, control evidence refreshes, operational metrics, and responses to assessor or agency review questions.

4
00:00:30.053 --> 00:00:31.902
The key concept is currency.

5
00:00:31.902 --> 00:00:35.370
Historical approval does not prove today's posture.

6
00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:37.990
New vulnerabilities may be discovered.

7
00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:39.839
Cloud resources may change.

8
00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:41.072
Access may drift.

9
00:00:41.072 --> 00:00:42.845
Incidents may reveal gaps.

10
00:00:42.845 --> 00:00:44.540
Dependencies may evolve.

11
00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:47.237
Controls may stop operating as intended.

12
00:00:47.237 --> 00:00:59.027
Continuous monitoring documentation helps stakeholders understand what has changed, what remains effective, what requires remediation, and what risk is being carried forward.

13
00:00:59.027 --> 00:01:06.039
Continuous monitoring records should align with the same scope and boundary story used in the core package.

14
00:01:06.039 --> 00:01:10.277
If the inventory changes, diagrams and evidence may need review.

15
00:01:10.277 --> 00:01:18.677
If a significant architectural change occurs, control narratives and inherited responsibility assumptions may need updates.

16
00:01:18.677 --> 00:01:25.381
If a vulnerability trend worsens, remediation documentation and risk reporting should reflect that.

17
00:01:25.381 --> 00:01:31.006
Monitoring evidence is only useful when it connects back to the system being assessed.

18
00:01:31.006 --> 00:01:35.861
The best continuous monitoring documentation reflects real operations.

19
00:01:35.861 --> 00:01:45.339
It should come from scans, tickets, logs, reviews, change records, incident processes, and control evidence refreshes that teams actually use.

20
00:01:45.339 --> 00:01:49.808
It should not be a historical snapshot copied forward indefinitely.

21
00:01:49.808 --> 00:01:59.441
The purpose is to keep security, compliance, engineering, and risk stakeholders aligned with the live system, not with last year's approval story.
